-- Static reference table: US county FIPS code -> representative
-- coordinate + timezone. Replaces the previous per-pole
-- Lat/Long + timezonefinder computation in pole_timezones_loader.py --
-- Poles.Lat/Poles.Long are frequently missing or incorrect, while
-- Poles.CountyFips (sourced from Airtable's "CountyFips" field) is
-- reliably populated. Switching to a county-level lookup does trade
-- away exact per-pole precision for reliability -- a handful of US
-- counties genuinely span more than one timezone, which this table
-- cannot represent (one row per FIPS code, one timezone each) -- but
-- this was an explicit, deliberate tradeoff given the alternative
-- (Lat/Long) was failing more often in practice than this coarser
-- county-level approximation would.
--
-- Coordinates are each county's Census-published centroid, EXCEPT for
-- three counties whose mathematical centroid falls in open water
-- (dispersed-island counties: Monroe County FL/Florida Keys, Aleutians
-- West Census Area AK, Honolulu County HI/Northwestern Hawaiian Islands
-- chain) -- these three were manually replaced with a named, land-based
-- population center within that same county (Key West, Adak, and the
-- city of Honolulu respectively), so both the coordinate and its
-- resulting timezone are actually representative of where a real pole
-- in that county could be. See CoordinateOverrideReason for exactly
-- which three rows this applies to.
--
-- Timezone for every row (including the three manual-coordinate ones
-- above) was computed the SAME way this project's own per-pole
-- resolution already did -- shared/timezone_utils.py's
-- resolve_windows_timezone(), backed by the timezonefinder library --
-- not a separately-sourced timezone dataset that could disagree with
-- how this project already classifies coordinates elsewhere.
--
-- CountyName capitalization: the source data (Census county centroids)
-- came all-lowercase -- title-cased here, including specific handling
-- for apostrophes (Prince George's, not Prince George'S), Mc-prefixed
-- names (McPherson), a handful of compound names with known internal
-- capitalization not derivable from any general rule (DeKalb, DeSoto,
-- DeWitt, DuPage, LaGrange, LaPorte, LaMoure, LaSalle -- verified
-- against each one's actual official spelling, not guessed), hyphenated
-- names (Miami-Dade), and short connector words that stay lowercase
-- mid-name per standard title-case convention and each area's own
-- official Census Bureau spelling (Isle of Wight, Prince of
-- Wales-Hyder, Lake of the Woods, District of Columbia, King and Queen,
-- Lewis and Clark, the Alaska "City and Borough" units, St. John the
-- Baptist).
--
-- FIPS is VARCHAR(5), not INT, for the same reason as Poles.CountyFips:
-- a FIPS code can have a leading zero (e.g. "01001" for Autauga County,
-- AL) that an INT column would silently strip.
IF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM sys.tables WHERE name = 'CountyTimeZones')
BEGIN
    CREATE TABLE CountyTimeZones (
        FIPS                     VARCHAR(5)    NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY,
        CountyName               NVARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,
        State                    VARCHAR(2)    NOT NULL,
        Latitude                 FLOAT         NOT NULL,
        Longitude                FLOAT         NOT NULL,
        IanaTimeZone             VARCHAR(50)   NOT NULL,
        WindowsTimeZone          VARCHAR(50)   NOT NULL,
        CoordinateOverrideReason NVARCHAR(200) NULL
    );
END
